Run the link watchdog

The watchdog of the Python reference was never ported: start_watchdog()
was empty and the loop sat beneath it as a comment. So a link request that
drew no proof stayed pending for ever, an initiator sent no keepalives, and
a link whose peer had gone was never found stale or closed.

The watchdog is now one pass of the reference loop, run for every pending
and active link by Transport::jobs(). With it come the pieces the loop
reads: the keepalive interval is derived from the round-trip time when a
link comes up, had_outbound() records the time of a keepalive, and a proved
link packet records the time of the proof.

It runs after the job loop has finished, not inside it, and the resource
watchdogs move there with it. Transport::outbound() waits for the job loop
to finish, so a packet sent from inside the loop waited for ever: a
resource watchdog asking again for a lost part would have hung the caller.

